thread:
becca: Flagging a blocker with the Zenith POCthey upload a ~5.5MB JSON bundle via presigned POST and get 413 from our edge. Demo is tomorrow AM.

alex: Oof. Is this presigned POST form (multipart/form-data) or presigned PUT?

becca: It's POST form. Client is a browser using FormData. Error body is an nginx HTML 413.

tina: Customer tried splitting client-side into two objects but their ingest pipeline expects a single context object. They asked if we can accept chunked streams or reassemble server-side.

ryan: Short answer: our CDN and nginx proxy have body limits for POST forms. Also some edges disallow large multipart bodies. I'll check config. @deploy-bot can you show the client_max_body_size and max_form_size for prod-edge?

deploy-bot: prod-edge config: client_max_body_size=6m, client_body_buffer_size=64k, max_form_fields=1000. Canary region prod-edge-us-west has client_max_body_size=12m.

alex: So the payload is ~5.5MB > 6m? Wait 5.5MB is less than 6m. Is the measured size possibly larger because browser adds multipart overhead?

becca: Good catch  browser multipart overhead pushes it to ~6.4MB. Also they attach a small image in same FormData which bumps total.

ryan: Options: (A) bump client_max_body_size on affected edges (requires infra rollout) (B) use S3 multipart upload with presigned parts (avoid edge body entirely) (C) have client gzip before upload and set Content-Encoding: gzip on the form field or upload gzipped file to S3 via presigned PUT (D) server-side assembler where client uploads multiple smaller objects and a final API call stitches them.

alex: Which option is fastest for a demo?

ryan: Fastest low-effort: route traffic for that org to us-west canary (already 12m) for the demo window. I can create a temporary route rule for their org-id that pins to prod-edge-us-west. Takes ~15m approved.

tina: Will check if customer is fine with being routed to us-west. They are in EU but ok for demo.

becca: For longer term we should give them an SDK pattern. I can write a tiny browser shim that does: 1) split large JSON into N chunks <= 2.5MB, 2) gzip each chunk, 3) presign PUT for each piece, 4) upload parts in parallel, 5) call /v1/context/assemble with list of piece keys. Include Content-Encoding header so ingestion can auto-unpack.

alex: Any gotchas re: token accounting when stitching? If we stitch server-side, do we recompute tokens only at assemble time? Customer worried about counting tokens twice and billing spikes.

ryan: We only bill tokens on final model requests. Storage and upload bytes are charged separately. When you stitch, the assembled object is what goes into the model; tokenization happens then. So no double token billing. Document it.

becca: Draft shim snippet (browser JS) to share in thread:

```
// pseudo
async function uploadChunks(json) {
  const chunks = splitJson(json, 2_400_000); // bytes per chunk
  const keys = [];
  for (const c of chunks) {
    const gz = await gzip(c);
    const presign = await fetch(`/v1/uploads/presign?size=${gz.byteLength}`);
    const {url, fields} = await presign.json();
    // if using presigned PUT
    await fetch(url, {method: 'PUT', body: gz, headers: {'Content-Encoding': 'gzip','Content-Type':'application/json'}});
    keys.push(fields.key || extractKeyFromUrl(url));
  }
  await fetch('/v1/context/assemble', {method:'POST', body: JSON.stringify({parts: keys})});
}
```

becca: Note: if you must use presigned POST form for some S3 setups, multipart upload (InitiateMultipartUpload + presigned parts) is the robust path.

alex: Can we also recommend the AWS SigV4 chunked upload for streaming huge bodies from clients? Is that supported by our presign flow?

ryan: We don't emit chunked-signed presigns today. It's on backlog. Implementing that would let clients stream large uploads without multipart, but it's more work and not available for the demo.

deploy-bot: Rolling temporary route rule for org:zenith-demo -> prod-edge-us-west. Pending manual approval by infra-ops.

tina: I told the customer the options and recommended gzip+presigned PUT or temporary route for demo. They picked route switch. Please confirm once live.

ryan: Route switch live on canary and promoted to requested edges. No errors observed. I'll follow up with PR to docs and a public example in our SDK repo demonstrating the chunk+assemble pattern.

alex: Perfect. Thanks all. After demo we'll push the browser shim to examples and add a note about Content-Encoding and multipart overhead. :thumbsup:
